<?xml version="1.0"?>
<?xml-model href="http://download.ros.org/schema/package_format3.xsd" schematypens="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema"?>
<package format="3">
  <name>waveshare_servos</name>
  <version>0.1.0</version>
  <description>ros2_control package for Waveshare ST servo motors</description>
  <maintainer email="jhat@uw.edu">lab</maintainer>
  <license>GPL-3.0-or-later</license>

  <buildtool_depend>ament_cmake</buildtool_depend>

  <depend>hardware_interface</depend>
  <depend>pluginlib</depend>
  <depend>rclcpp</depend>
  <depend>rclcpp_lifecycle</depend>

  <test_depend>ament_cmake_gmock</test_depend>
  <test_depend>ament_lint_auto</test_depend>
  <test_depend>ament_lint_common</test_depend>
  <test_depend>ros2_control_test_assets</test_depend>

  <!-- test/hil/hil_record.py, the recorder of the hil_check bench test, imports all five of
       these, and only when that test actually runs against hardware.

       Two of them have a SECOND user since Phase 5, and it is not hardware-gated: rclpy is
       imported at module scope by test/test_example_launch.py (it creates the node the service
       calls there reuse), and sensor_msgs is imported at module scope by
       controller_manager.test_utils (test_utils.py:34, `from sensor_msgs.msg import
       JointState`), which that same test imports. So rclpy and sensor_msgs are needed by every
       run of the launch test, motors or no motors; dropping them because hil_check skips would
       break test_example_launch at its first import on a clean checkout. control_msgs,
       std_msgs and trajectory_msgs are still hil_record.py's alone. -->
  <test_depend>control_msgs</test_depend>
  <test_depend>rclpy</test_depend>
  <test_depend>sensor_msgs</test_depend>
  <test_depend>std_msgs</test_depend>
  <test_depend>trajectory_msgs</test_depend>

  <!-- The two motorless tests added in Phase 5: test/test_urdf_xacro.py, which renders the
       example description with xacro and reads the XML back, and test/test_example_launch.py,
       which brings the example up on mock hardware. ament_cmake_pytest and
       launch_testing_ament_cmake provide the CMake functions that register the two
       (ament_add_pytest_test, add_launch_test); launch_testing and launch are imported by the
       launch test, ament_index_python by both, and xacro by both as well (the launch test's
       pre-flight guard renders the description before it will let the launch start).
       launch_testing_ros is imported by neither, but controller_manager.test_utils imports it at
       module scope (test_utils.py:33, `from launch_testing_ros import WaitForTopics`), so the
       launch test's very first import fails without it. xacro is exec_depend below as well; it is
       repeated here because it is the one dependency a test needs that nothing in the build or
       the runtime pulls in on its own, and the duplication costs nothing.

       launch is listed even though launch_testing already depends on it, because a test that
       writes `from launch import LaunchDescription` should declare launch. The usual objection
       to adding a test_depend does not apply here, and this was measured rather than assumed:
       ament_lint_auto_find_test_dependencies() calls find_package(${dep} QUIET) for every
       <test_depend> (ament_lint_auto_find_test_dependencies.cmake:35-40), and `launch` is an
       ament_python package that installs no CMake config at all (there is no
       /opt/ros/jazzy/share/launch/cmake), so that call finds nothing, says nothing and leaves
       launch_FOUND unset; a probe project configured with find_package(ament_cmake REQUIRED)
       followed by find_package(launch QUIET) printed launch_FOUND=0 with a 0-byte stderr, the
       same as the launch_testing entry already here. Contrast controller_manager below, which
       does ship a config and does cost a warning.

       controller_manager is DELIBERATELY NOT REPEATED HERE, though test/test_example_launch.py
       imports it. ament_lint_auto_find_test_dependencies() find_package()es every <test_depend>
       (ament_lint_auto_find_test_dependencies.cmake:35-40), and find_package(controller_manager)
       reaches tl_expected through parameter_traits, which prints a CMake deprecation warning.
       Measured, not assumed: adding the line takes this package's build stderr.log from 0 bytes
       to that warning, and removing it takes it back to 0 bytes. jazzy.md makes a warning-free
       build a phase gate, and the dependency is already covered anyway: controller_manager is an
       <exec_depend> below, and `rosdep install` installs every dependency type except doc by
       default, so a clean checkout resolves it regardless. Re-adding it buys nothing and costs
       the gate. -->
  <test_depend>ament_cmake_pytest</test_depend>
  <test_depend>ament_index_python</test_depend>
  <test_depend>launch</test_depend>
  <test_depend>launch_testing</test_depend>
  <test_depend>launch_testing_ament_cmake</test_depend>
  <test_depend>launch_testing_ros</test_depend>
  <test_depend>xacro</test_depend>

  <exec_depend>controller_manager</exec_depend>
  <exec_depend>diff_drive_controller</exec_depend>
  <exec_depend>joint_state_broadcaster</exec_depend>
  <exec_depend>joint_trajectory_controller</exec_depend>
  <exec_depend>robot_state_publisher</exec_depend>
  <exec_depend>ros2controlcli</exec_depend>
  <exec_depend>ros2launch</exec_depend>
  <exec_depend>rviz2</exec_depend>
  <exec_depend>velocity_controllers</exec_depend>
  <exec_depend>xacro</exec_depend>

  <export>
    <build_type>ament_cmake</build_type>
  </export>
</package>
